.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="The volumetric analysis Gay-Lussac and Mohr systematized finds how much acid a sample contains from the burette volume at which it has been exactly neutralized -- the equivalence point, seen by eye as an indicator&#x27;s sudden colour change and here located numerically as the steepest point of the pH curve (~chemistrykit.solutions.core.base_system.Titration.find_equivalence_point). Comparing a strong-acid/strong-base titration (~chemistrykit.solutions.systems.titration.StrongAcidStrongBaseTitration) against a weak-acid/strong-base titration (~chemistrykit.solutions.systems.titration.WeakAcidStrongBaseTitration) of the same concentrations: the weak-acid curve starts at a higher pH, has a buffer region around its half-equivalence point (where pH = pKa), and its equivalence point is basic rather than neutral -- all visible directly from the curve shapes.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/solutions/titration/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_01_titration_curves_thumb.png
